00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.340
I know I have relationships,
I know people.

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:21.090
And I know what they're kind of
looking for, or they know me.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:22.980
So I might just
sidestep this process.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.830
And in the end,
what happens there?

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:29.100
Something that we designed
to keep us secure is now,

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.200
people have found a
loophole and found

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:33.220
the path of least resistance.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:34.630
So this is a real bad problem.

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:36.270
So it looks like
there's no good way

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:37.620
to solve this, right, Ruben?

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.210
I mean, I think
that's the challenge

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:40.290
of where we are today.

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:41.040
RUBEN GONZALEZ:
I mean, there are

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.170
different in the ways of
how we can address this.

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:47.370
We can see this as a
process-driven solution,

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:51.600
or perhaps making a mix,
including as part of the mix

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:54.120
the right tool for you to be
able to address these issues.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:56.100
RAYMOND PENG: Yeah, and so
before we get into tools--

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.660
I think as
technologists, we always

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:59.160
think about how
can we use tools?

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:02.040
I think the most important thing
that we find with our customers

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:05.970
and organizations, it's
really a mindset shift first.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.670
And so at Apigee, we call
it API Governance 2.0.

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:11.130
And we wrote this manifesto
working with hundreds

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:12.670
of customers worldwide.

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:14.930
And we've seen
their programs grow

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:16.500
based on this kind of mindset.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:19.890
And so the first one is really
around empowering and enabling

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:21.150
over enforcement.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:24.030
How can I help my API
teams, give them the tools,

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:28.320
give them knowledge, give them
the ability to make decisions?

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.430
Then the second is guidance
and support over policing.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:35.470
The third is accelerating
progress over slowing change.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:37.630
So really it's a dynamic.

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:41.830
I'm not focused on how can I
overturn everything and look

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:42.330
at it?

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:44.580
I really want to focus on speed.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:47.490
What can I do to keep
these things going fast?

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:50.620
And last is this relinquishing
of a centralized control.

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:53.850
So trust and federation
over centralized control.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:55.950
I think trust is not
something that you can just

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:57.570
basically say trust me.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:00.190
You have to build
trust into your system.

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:01.860
So that's the
cornerstone, I think,

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:06.330
of the mindset change, this
manifesto of Governance 2.0.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:08.530
And so that really
means a lot of teams,

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:10.920
they call them API
centers of Excellence.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:13.020
But what we want
people to shift to

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:15.540
is this API center
of enablement.

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:17.440
And so what does that look like?

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.710
I think really quickly,
Big G governance

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:21.420
versus little g culture.

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:24.090
That's really the shift in
terms of Big G governance

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:25.770
to this Governance 2.0.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:28.500
It means I'm going from
rules, gates, and enforcements

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:31.080
to principles, standards,
and empowerment.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:34.169
I used to have top-down design
and planning and big waterfall.

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:35.700
Now I have separation
of concerns.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:37.380
I have an enterprise concern.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:39.760
I allow teams to do
what they need to do.

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:41.970
I'm going from
approvals for releases

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.220
to criteria-based and
continuous releases.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:48.120
I want people to understand
what it means to pass Go

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:51.090
and to hit the criteria so
that they can always release

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:52.830
constantly at their speed.

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:55.530
And I'm not going to do
manual and analog processes.

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.960
I'm going to look at how
I can create self-service

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:00.360
through automation and tooling.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:04.980
And I'm going to enforce
community knowledge

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:07.660
and community documentation,
rather than centralized Word

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:08.610
documents.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:10.153
And then the last couple, too.

00:09:10.153 --> 00:09:11.820
I'm going to have
cross-functional teams

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:12.540
and coaches.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:15.870
I'm not going to have distinct
big handovers from teams, which

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:18.270
is very core to the
way of agile thinking,

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:20.460
the same thing as
Governance 2.0.

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:23.370
And instead of spending all
this time aligning and building

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:28.200
corporate politics, I'm going to
focus on building tools, CI/CD,

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:31.020
to help the teams go faster
so that they're essentially

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:34.770
using efficiency based
tools rather than alignment

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:37.710
and time spent on wasted
on meetings and politics.

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:41.340
If I have that, I have this
API Governance 2.0, Ruben.

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:43.950
Let's take a look at the
process that we have today.

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:47.760
There's four points, API
architecture, design,

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:50.980
standards, and then
deployment and live.

00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:54.390
So how can we use
technology in this case, how

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:57.630
can we apply the principles
of API 2.0 to all four

00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:59.760
of these things?

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:02.172
Let's do that and try to
solve this problem together.

00:10:02.172 --> 00:10:03.630
RUBEN GONZALEZ:
Definitely, so from

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:05.700
an architectural
standpoint, perhaps you

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:09.930
might want to look into a
way to standardize globally

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:11.730
and across all your
existing architecture.

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:14.010
It doesn't matter
if you are deploying

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:16.830
on a cloud or multiple cloud
providers or even on premises.

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:19.800
You want to build a
layer of abstraction

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:21.570
on top of your
existing services so

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:25.110
that you can enforce and not
only enforce, but decouple

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:27.060
common security patterns
from your existing

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:30.958
system, supporting
your existing APIs so

00:10:30.958 --> 00:10:32.500
that you're going
to be able to build

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:33.750
this layer of abstraction.

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:35.910
And essentially,
this will become

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:39.270
a shield that is going to
protect both the interactions

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:41.790
from your existing
API consumers and also

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:44.640
the secure interactions between
this platform and your back end

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:45.330
systems.

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:47.410
By setting up this
layer of abstraction,

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:49.920
you're going to be able to
standardize all your security

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:53.053
patterns and make sure that
they are enforced correctly.

00:10:53.053 --> 00:10:53.970
RAYMOND PENG: Perfect.

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:57.630
So if I'm an API
developer, I deploy an API

00:10:57.630 --> 00:11:01.470
to the API platform,
I'm automatically

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.330
going to gain the
benefits of threat

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:05.580
protection, the security,
and the patterns

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:06.670
that you're talking about.

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:11.637
So I'm essentially deploying
on a trusted platform.

00:11:11.637 --> 00:11:12.970
RUBEN GONZALEZ: That is correct.

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:15.750
So essentially you're
building secure interactions

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:18.120
between your API consumers
and your back end systems

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:22.500
through a standardized shield or
layer of abstraction in an API

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:23.220
Platform.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:25.140
RAYMOND PENG: What are some of
the examples of the patterns

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:26.620
that you're talking
about, Ruben?

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:28.500
RUBEN GONZALEZ: So perhaps
from a network perspective,

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.483
you're going to be building
trust relationships

00:11:30.483 --> 00:11:32.400
within the platform and
your back end systems.

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:34.980
Perhaps you're already using
an existing security pattern.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:36.660
So the only thing
you need to do is

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:40.167
to set up a really simple trust
relationship between the API

00:11:40.167 --> 00:11:42.000
platform and your
existing back end systems.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:45.960
Perhaps you can build up a
very simple mutual TLS setup.

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:49.140
Perhaps if you're looking into
token based authentication

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:51.750
mechanisms, that's something
you can build upon.

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:54.690
However, if you want to
reuse what you already have,

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:55.710
you can do it.

00:11:55.710 --> 00:11:58.740
And then you can keep
progressing those patterns

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:01.170
so that you can
start standardizing

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:03.240
once new projects come in.

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:07.920
And the same story goes on
the consumer relationship.

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:09.480
Perhaps your
organization might be

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:12.420
thinking on setting up OAuth
as a standard across the board.

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:15.298
That's something that you
can enforce at this layer.

00:12:15.298 --> 00:12:16.590
RAYMOND PENG: Perfect, perfect.

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:19.560
So I've got my API
management setup now.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:21.850
I've got the security
architecture in place.

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:24.190
You talked a little bit
about OAuth and security.

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:27.000
Can you tell me a little bit
more about-- now let's dive in.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:28.485
I've got a couple of APIs.

00:12:28.485 --> 00:12:29.610
RUBEN GONZALEZ: Definitely.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:31.970
So usually from a
development standpoint--

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:33.600
this happens too often.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:36.990
Each and every time you have
to expose a new service,

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:40.200
we developers need to
sometimes get too creative

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:42.780
and start setting up new
security pattern, or new ways

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:45.165
to secure our project or APIs.

00:12:45.165 --> 00:12:48.120
And for each and every project,
we have to set up that pattern.

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:50.860
However, there's a
best way to do it.

00:12:50.860 --> 00:12:52.830
So once you build this
layer of abstraction,

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:56.310
perhaps you can also start
decoupling this pattern

00:12:56.310 --> 00:13:00.240
so that no longer is it going
to be the developer's task

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:02.760
to have to come up with
a new security pattern.

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:05.700
Then essentially after
decoupling these patterns,

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:09.480
you're essentially creating
shareable libraries.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:13.440
And in this case, you can
decouple an authentication

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:16.980
and authorization shared
library through a policy.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:20.880
And now the developer doesn't
have to come up with all this.

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:23.310
And then he can really
focus on the business value

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:25.410
that he's going to be
exposing through an API.

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:26.202
RAYMOND PENG: Yeah.

00:13:26.202 --> 00:13:29.280
We love creativity, but not when
it comes to security, right?

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:30.853
RUBEN GONZALEZ: That is correct.

00:13:30.853 --> 00:13:33.270
RAYMOND PENG: We want to do
best practice and standardize.

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:34.530
RUBEN GONZALEZ: Exactly.

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:37.235
And remember, security's
not all about just

00:13:37.235 --> 00:13:38.610
authentication
and authorization.

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:42.120
Perhaps you want to protect
your APIs against DDoS attacks,

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:43.980
or perhaps against
content-based attacks.

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:46.290
That's something
that, again, is great

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:49.060
that you're thinking of
on those different issues.

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:52.390
However, you just need to have
to come up with those patterns

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:56.610
once, and perhaps
the person in charge

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:59.190
of designing or
identifying those patterns

00:13:59.190 --> 00:14:01.470
is a completely
different stakeholder

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:04.500
than from a web
developer standpoint.

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:06.000
Perhaps within your
own organization

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:07.680
you already have a
security team that

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:11.970
is in charge of making sure
these patterns are applied

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:13.415
correctly throughout
all the APIs

00:14:13.415 --> 00:14:14.790
that you're going
to be exposing.

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:17.790
So you will need to
have the right tool that

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:20.730
will enable you to have
the separation of duties.

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:24.630
The developer team, the API team
will focus on the real business

00:14:24.630 --> 00:14:27.630
value that we're going to
be exposing through APIs.

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:29.460
And of course, the
security stakeholders

00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:31.530
are going to treat
these shared security

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:34.620
policies as a
non-functional requirement.

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:36.240
Although they are
very important,

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:38.037
you just need to come
up with them once

00:14:38.037 --> 00:14:39.870
and then you don't have
to worry about them.

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:41.910
You can keep
redeploying new APIs

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:45.000
and you don't need to do that
all the revalidation process

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:47.240
for these specific
aspects of your releases.

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:48.990
RAYMOND PENG: So it's
almost like the path

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:49.860
of least resistance.

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:52.410
If I'm a developer,
and I'm creating APIs,

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:55.290
I mean, you're actually making
my life a little easier.

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:57.060
I just got to focus
on the request

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:00.010
and response of the payload
and the business logic there.

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:02.510
But all the stuff around
the shared security policies

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:05.240
is no longer a thing that I
have to be concerned with.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:08.090
If I deploy on the
API platform, I'm

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:11.370
automatically getting some
of these policies for free.

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:12.900
They're added already, right?

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:14.233
RUBEN GONZALEZ: That is correct.

00:15:14.233 --> 00:15:16.700
And still, you might want
to have the right tool just

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:18.265
because you will
still want to have

00:15:18.265 --> 00:15:20.030
a certain level of flexibility.

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:24.680
Perhaps you're going to come up
with specific parameters that

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:31.400
are going to be able to let the
API team to decide which rate

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:33.970
or quotas you're going to
be applying for API or API

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:34.470
product.

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:37.600
So again, you still want to
have that separation of concerns

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:39.260
in regards of
policy enforcement.

00:15:39.260 --> 00:15:40.820
However, there
are certain levels

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:43.880
of parameterizing
options you might

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:47.450
want to leave to be the
responsibility of the API

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:49.313
team or even the business team.

00:15:49.313 --> 00:15:50.105
RAYMOND PENG: Nice.

00:15:50.105 --> 00:15:51.230
So I get that flexibility.

00:15:51.230 --> 00:15:54.830
And also, anybody who uses the
platform says, hey, security's

00:15:54.830 --> 00:15:55.850
already been involved.

00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:58.160
They've actually been
part of the architecture

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:00.290
of the policies even
that are being added,

00:16:00.290 --> 00:16:01.370
which is really great.

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:03.912
RUBEN GONZALEZ: Remember, we're
talking about artifacts here.

00:16:03.912 --> 00:16:07.220
So every software
product nowadays

00:16:07.220 --> 00:16:09.590
has to go through a software
development lifecycle.

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:12.050
And the best way to
do all these releases

00:16:12.050 --> 00:16:13.820
is through an agile process.

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:17.875
So thinking in terms of an
API platform and everything

00:16:17.875 --> 00:16:20.000
that you're going to be
deploying within this tool,

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:22.130
we're thinking in
configuration terms.

00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:26.030
So usually, we have to train
these configuration artifacts

00:16:26.030 --> 00:16:29.720
as if they were any
other software artifact.

00:16:29.720 --> 00:16:32.960
So they're going to
have a release process.

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:36.860
They're going to have specific
stages where you might want

00:16:36.860 --> 00:16:39.230
to run some quality checks.

00:16:39.230 --> 00:16:42.050
And you're going to
want to make sure

00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:46.970
that you're applying and
making sure the right security

00:16:46.970 --> 00:16:49.250
policies are being enforced
even before deploying

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:51.890
this configuration
into the platform.

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:53.840
After these
configuration artifacts

00:16:53.840 --> 00:16:55.520
are deployed into
the platform, you

00:16:55.520 --> 00:16:59.690
want to make sure that even
though they were already

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:02.130
pre-validated, once
you deploy them,

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:07.609
you can re-run some tests to be
able to have a full integration

00:17:07.609 --> 00:17:10.280
test results, from
the consumer side

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:11.839
all the way to your
back end systems.

00:17:11.839 --> 00:17:15.619
So again, you will have
even multiple security--

00:17:15.619 --> 00:17:17.450
this kind of
lifecycle will enable

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:20.690
you to have multiple security
checks before deploying,

00:17:20.690 --> 00:17:21.920
and even after deploying.

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:23.089
RAYMOND PENG: Nice,
so there's a lot

00:17:23.089 --> 00:17:25.550
of tools in the CI/CD pipeline
that you're talking about,

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:26.150
right?

00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:27.525
RUBEN GONZALEZ:
Yeah, definitely.

00:17:27.525 --> 00:17:30.750
And the beauty of it is that
every customer is different,

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:32.450
and as well every
customer might be

00:17:32.450 --> 00:17:36.530
in a different stage in
regards of their maturity level

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:39.050
on using agile
software development

00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:40.580
lifecycle and pipelines.

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:45.470
So most organizations want to be
able to reuse what they already

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:46.760
got.

00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:48.650
You don't want to
get a platform that

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:51.230
is going to force you to
choose a specific set of tools.

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:52.882
You want to have flexibility.

00:17:52.882 --> 00:17:54.840
And that's great from a
development standpoint.

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:56.510
You don't need to
rethink the wheel.

00:17:56.510 --> 00:18:00.200
If you already used to handle
