authentication.

00:26:15.230 --> 00:26:19.220
Perhaps you may also want
to restrict and protect

00:26:19.220 --> 00:26:21.650
your back-end systems against
content-based attacks.

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:25.550
And these two next policies
address exactly that.

00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:28.250
So this first one protects
your back end systems

00:26:28.250 --> 00:26:30.170
against SQL injection attacks.

00:26:30.170 --> 00:26:32.960
And the second one is
protecting your back-end systems

00:26:32.960 --> 00:26:37.060
against code injection attacks.

00:26:37.060 --> 00:26:37.880
RAYMOND PENG: Nice.

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:39.047
RUBEN GONZALEZ: Makes sense?

00:26:39.047 --> 00:26:39.830
So now that I--

00:26:39.830 --> 00:26:44.000
me, as a security stakeholder,
defined this shared function

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:46.160
within my organization,
then there's

00:26:46.160 --> 00:26:49.550
an extra way I can make sure
that no one can do anything

00:26:49.550 --> 00:26:51.560
about it and it's going
to be enforced globally.

00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:55.220
And for that, we're going to
be using our global environment

00:26:55.220 --> 00:26:56.570
configuration.

00:26:56.570 --> 00:26:58.130
And within our
environments, we're

00:26:58.130 --> 00:27:05.360
going to have what
we call flow hooks.

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:08.310
So there are three
main flow hooks

00:27:08.310 --> 00:27:12.510
in an environment within the
Apigee Edge organization.

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:16.160
So essentially, flow
hooks are execution points

00:27:16.160 --> 00:27:19.082
for the interactions between the
API consumer and the platform,

00:27:19.082 --> 00:27:21.290
and of course, the platform
and the back end systems.

00:27:21.290 --> 00:27:24.950
But these execution points
are exactly at the edges,

00:27:24.950 --> 00:27:30.470
meaning the developer will not
be able to touch these edges.

00:27:30.470 --> 00:27:33.650
These are policies and sections
that are enforced globally.

00:27:33.650 --> 00:27:36.007
OK, so I'm going to
switch environment here.

00:27:36.007 --> 00:27:38.090
I'm going to switch to my
development environment.

00:27:38.090 --> 00:27:41.270
And as you can see,
well, here it is.

00:27:41.270 --> 00:27:43.580
My global enterprise
security policy

00:27:43.580 --> 00:27:46.070
is being enforced
at the pre-proxy.

00:27:46.070 --> 00:27:48.590
The pre-proxy flow
hook is guaranteed

00:27:48.590 --> 00:27:51.750
to execute before the
transaction reaches your API

00:27:51.750 --> 00:27:52.250
proxy.

00:27:52.250 --> 00:27:57.080
Therefore, the API
developer will not

00:27:57.080 --> 00:28:00.033
be able to manage or
to change this setting.

00:28:00.033 --> 00:28:00.825
RAYMOND PENG: Nice.

00:28:00.825 --> 00:28:03.470
So any API deployed to
the test environment

00:28:03.470 --> 00:28:06.200
is going to go through these
enterprise security policies

00:28:06.200 --> 00:28:08.150
that you just said
in the pre-proxy.

00:28:08.150 --> 00:28:08.715
RUBEN GONZALEZ: That is correct.

00:28:08.715 --> 00:28:11.090
RAYMOND PENG: And I don't have
