00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:48.110
OK, so now a common use case
for a security stakeholder

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:51.230
is going to be, well, I'm going
to be interested in failed

00:29:51.230 --> 00:29:52.350
authentication attempts.

00:29:52.350 --> 00:29:54.800
I'm going to make sure that,
well, I'm not being attacked

00:29:54.800 --> 00:29:59.640
or a malicious user is
not trying to overwhelm

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:03.660
my authorization services.

00:30:03.660 --> 00:30:06.290
So in this case, I'm going
to create a new alert.

00:30:06.290 --> 00:30:07.940
In this case, this
alert is going

00:30:07.940 --> 00:30:10.265
to be called
Unauthorized Access.

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:15.200
OK.

00:30:15.200 --> 00:30:17.860
It's going to be deployed
in the test environment.

00:30:17.860 --> 00:30:20.595
OK, and I'm going to
set up some conditions.

00:30:20.595 --> 00:30:22.970
So initially, we're going to
set up a basic metric, which

00:30:22.970 --> 00:30:24.470
is going to be status code.

00:30:24.470 --> 00:30:27.410
And me as the
security stakeholder,

00:30:27.410 --> 00:30:32.570
I'm going to be very interested
in 401s, because this error

00:30:32.570 --> 00:30:35.180
relates to unauthorized access.

00:30:35.180 --> 00:30:36.522
Now let's define a threshold.

00:30:36.522 --> 00:30:38.480
In this case, I'm going
to define the threshold

00:30:38.480 --> 00:30:39.590
as Account.

00:30:39.590 --> 00:30:45.500
And I'm only going to allow
10 unauthorized attempts

00:30:45.500 --> 00:30:48.020
every five minutes.

00:30:48.020 --> 00:30:50.750
I'm going to add
an extra dimension.

00:30:50.750 --> 00:30:53.510
In this case, I'm going to
select the proxy, the API

00:30:53.510 --> 00:30:55.850
proxies as my dimension.

00:30:55.850 --> 00:31:00.350
And this condition is going to
be applied to all available API

00:31:00.350 --> 00:31:01.800
proxies within an environment.

00:31:01.800 --> 00:31:05.270
Remember, ultimately
the API platform

00:31:05.270 --> 00:31:07.940
sits on top of all your
existing back end systems.

00:31:07.940 --> 00:31:11.233
It doesn't matter where
they are deployed.

00:31:11.233 --> 00:31:12.650
So you want the
right tool for you

00:31:12.650 --> 00:31:15.950
to be able to manage these
flexible deployments.

00:31:15.950 --> 00:31:20.510
In this case, I'm able
to scope down conditions

00:31:20.510 --> 00:31:21.840
to the API level.

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:24.200
However, this
condition is a global--

00:31:24.200 --> 00:31:27.290
I'm sorry, this
filter is a global one

00:31:27.290 --> 00:31:29.420
that is going to be
applied throughout all

00:31:29.420 --> 00:31:31.740
the available APIs
in the environment.

00:31:31.740 --> 00:31:35.270
And also, it's going to applied
throughout all available

00:31:35.270 --> 00:31:37.050
regions within my organization.

00:31:37.050 --> 00:31:38.480
Remember, you want
a solution that

00:31:38.480 --> 00:31:41.850
can be deployed in
multiple environments.

00:31:41.850 --> 00:31:44.970
OK, so now that I
set up my conditions,

00:31:44.970 --> 00:31:49.440
I'm going to be able to
create notification channels.

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:54.870
Within my alert, I can define
multiple notification channels.

00:31:54.870 --> 00:31:59.640
In this case, we support
four main channels, which

00:31:59.640 --> 00:32:01.750
is going to be email, Slack--

00:32:01.750 --> 00:32:04.200
it's a very common tool
that nowadays DevOps teams

00:32:04.200 --> 00:32:05.633
use all the time.

00:32:05.633 --> 00:32:07.050
If you already
have PagerDuty, you

00:32:07.050 --> 00:32:08.342
can integrate that very easily.

00:32:08.342 --> 00:32:11.670
Or even if you have a
completely different set up,

00:32:11.670 --> 00:32:14.770
or custom setup for you to be
able to be notified, of course,

00:32:14.770 --> 00:32:17.270
