You can make them easy
for teams to add, as well.

00:44:57.945 --> 00:45:00.320
And there's a lot of flexibility
to go in and instantiate

00:45:00.320 --> 00:45:02.090
different variables
into policies.

00:45:02.090 --> 00:45:02.630
RUBEN GONZALEZ: Exactly.

00:45:02.630 --> 00:45:04.463
You can either give the
option to developers

00:45:04.463 --> 00:45:06.710
to actually enforce that
policy if you want to

00:45:06.710 --> 00:45:08.600
and do some checks
before deployment.

00:45:08.600 --> 00:45:10.412
Or you can enforce
them globally.

00:45:10.412 --> 00:45:12.120
Again, there are
different ways to do it.

00:45:12.120 --> 00:45:14.360
But the beauty of the
Apigee Edge platform

00:45:14.360 --> 00:45:16.630
is that you do
have those options.

00:45:16.630 --> 00:45:19.090
You can build your
own custom way

00:45:19.090 --> 00:45:21.710
of enforcing these policies, and
onboarding your developers so

00:45:21.710 --> 00:45:25.100
that they understand how
they're being applied.

00:45:25.100 --> 00:45:26.120
RAYMOND PENG: OK, great.

00:45:26.120 --> 00:45:28.190
I see another question here.

00:45:28.190 --> 00:45:30.200
I think we've covered
it, but I think

00:45:30.200 --> 00:45:32.630
it's a really good question
just to reinforce again.

00:45:32.630 --> 00:45:35.120
The question is, does
Apigee provide any tools

00:45:35.120 --> 00:45:39.625
for CI/CD, code quality,
or test automation?

00:45:39.625 --> 00:45:41.500
RUBEN GONZALEZ: So yeah,
remember, everything

00:45:41.500 --> 00:45:44.020
that you saw today in
the demo, everything

00:45:44.020 --> 00:45:48.200
from the clicks I did, from the
developer review, everything,

00:45:48.200 --> 00:45:49.820
there's an API behind it.

00:45:49.820 --> 00:45:51.910
So the Apigee Edge
platform has been

00:45:51.910 --> 00:45:54.220
built for it to be extensible.

00:45:54.220 --> 00:45:56.590
So our customers
use Maven plugins

00:45:56.590 --> 00:45:59.050
for them to be able to deploy
configuration artifacts.

00:45:59.050 --> 00:46:02.020
They use JavaScript
tools to deploy, and also

00:46:02.020 --> 00:46:04.720
to test the custom
code that they

00:46:04.720 --> 00:46:06.130
deploy within the platform.

00:46:06.130 --> 00:46:08.640
So out there, you will
have multiple options.

00:46:08.640 --> 00:46:11.110
And of course, we also have
a very strong community

00:46:11.110 --> 00:46:12.010
of developers.

00:46:12.010 --> 00:46:16.420
All our customers are
always engaging with us

00:46:16.420 --> 00:46:18.970
and also setting up some
questions in our Apigee

00:46:18.970 --> 00:46:19.660
community.

00:46:19.660 --> 00:46:22.680
And of course, there's a
lot of knowledge out there.

00:46:22.680 --> 00:46:27.400
And a lot of open [INAUDIBLE]
of solutions that even

00:46:27.400 --> 00:46:29.140
between customers,
they start sharing

00:46:29.140 --> 00:46:30.370
and reusing and contributing.

00:46:30.370 --> 00:46:31.840
RAYMOND PENG: Perfect.

00:46:31.840 --> 00:46:34.300
It's certainly a broad
community to leverage

00:46:34.300 --> 00:46:37.060
within Apigee and outside.

00:46:37.060 --> 00:46:39.760
So number four, in
terms of question,

00:46:39.760 --> 00:46:42.580
how big is the center
of enablement team?

00:46:42.580 --> 00:46:44.740
First of all, thank you
for using that language,

00:46:44.740 --> 00:46:47.440
and starting to
think about that.

00:46:47.440 --> 00:46:49.300
Center of enablement
team, we see, varies.

00:46:49.300 --> 00:46:52.210
But we generally
see a small group

00:46:52.210 --> 00:46:55.360
within the central team
focused on evangelism,

00:46:55.360 --> 00:46:58.250
setting standards, and
coaching and supporting others.

00:46:58.250 --> 00:47:01.603
We're looking at probably a
handful of people in terms

00:47:01.603 --> 00:47:02.770
of the center of enablement.

00:47:02.770 --> 00:47:06.790
From there, we see it varying
in terms of how many API

00:47:06.790 --> 00:47:08.080
teams you're supporting.

00:47:08.080 --> 00:47:11.690
If you've got 10 or
hundreds of API teams,

00:47:11.690 --> 00:47:13.600
obviously, your
center of enablement's

00:47:13.600 --> 00:47:16.510
probably going to have to
scale up, but over time.

00:47:16.510 --> 00:47:19.840
So I don't think this is
about creating a big team.

00:47:19.840 --> 00:47:22.930
It's really creating a central
team to set the standards

00:47:22.930 --> 00:47:27.760
and start to coach and enable
other teams to grow quickly.

00:47:27.760 --> 00:47:30.960
And there's-- I think this is
the last question we've gotten.

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:34.600
So it's a great
question to end with.

00:47:34.600 --> 00:47:37.660
The question is, do I need
a dedicated security person

00:47:37.660 --> 00:47:39.100
on the API governance team?

00:47:39.100 --> 00:47:40.215
What do you think, Ruben?

00:47:40.215 --> 00:47:42.340
RUBEN GONZALEZ: That's a
very interesting question.

00:47:42.340 --> 00:47:44.932
Most likely, there's someone
within your organization,

00:47:44.932 --> 00:47:46.390
and you're going
to get to meet him

00:47:46.390 --> 00:47:49.176
once you try to deploy
something into production.

00:47:52.300 --> 00:47:55.810
And again, this varies as well
on the sizes of organizations.

00:47:55.810 --> 00:47:59.560
However, sometimes if
your team is too small

00:47:59.560 --> 00:48:01.750
or the organization is too
small, then most likely

00:48:01.750 --> 00:48:04.580
the developer will share
that responsibility.

00:48:04.580 --> 00:48:06.290
However, the best
practice is, of course,

00:48:06.290 --> 00:48:09.630
from a security standpoint, to
have that separation of duties,

00:48:09.630 --> 00:48:13.540
so that then someone can focus
only on the right security

00:48:13.540 --> 00:48:16.390
patterns, and the developers
can focus on the real business

00:48:16.390 --> 00:48:17.770
value that you want to expose.

00:48:17.770 --> 00:48:19.390
RAYMOND PENG: Yeah.

00:48:19.390 --> 00:48:21.520
So if we have that
separation of concerns,

00:48:21.520 --> 00:48:24.550
I'd say when you start out with
setting up your API management

00:48:24.550 --> 00:48:26.620
platform, this is
the perfect time

00:48:26.620 --> 00:48:29.200
to invite the security
person to the table.

00:48:29.200 --> 00:48:32.080
Let's not meet them at the
very end, like Ruben said.

00:48:32.080 --> 00:48:34.960
I think let's make friends
from the very beginning

00:48:34.960 --> 00:48:38.560
and have security at the table,
designing and architecting

00:48:38.560 --> 00:48:40.630
the platform with us
together, and then

00:48:40.630 --> 00:48:43.990
build those policies and
the approach and quality

00:48:43.990 --> 00:48:46.570
into the platform and
into basically every API

00:48:46.570 --> 00:48:49.710
[INAUDIBLE] deployed.

00:48:49.710 --> 00:48:54.250
And so with that, I think
that's the end of our webcast.

00:48:54.250 --> 00:48:57.810
I hope you guys got a
lot out of this webcast.

00:48:57.810 --> 00:48:59.910
And thank you all
for taking the time

00:48:59.910 --> 00:49:03.100
and joining with
Ruben and I today.

00:49:03.100 --> 00:49:04.300
Thank you all.

00:49:04.300 --> 00:49:05.800
RUBEN GONZALEZ:
Thank you all, guys.

